(5536364, 798000, 81530, '<td valign="top">Congrats on your first ribbon.</td>', 'Congrats on your first ribbon.', '2009-06-19 11:19:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(5536470, 797746, 96721, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/> <br/>For me the sunset area is a little too low in the scene and I think this would have looked really nice had the camera angle been slightly lower, that would have resulted in the darker area at the top being out of the frame too which i think would have added to the image, I think the Hexagon shapes are amazing and with that sunset in the background there was the making of a seriously good challenge entry here (although I think it might be a stretch for Architecture for the voters on this site).<br/> <br/>Camera Work:<br/> <br/>Everything looks to be in order, nice use of high ISO to get the shot and well retained details in the sunset.<br/> <br/>Post-Processing:<br/> <br/>Not too heavily saturated which is good as I suspect it would have been very easy to get carried away processing that sky.<br/> <br/>My Opinion:<br/> <br/>Nice out of the box thinking and I reckon in the right challenge this location has the potential to produce a shot that could finish very high, there''s some lovely shapes formed making for a very interesting capture. This one probably fell a bit in score due to the subject matter and the voters expecting to see a building or very specific types of structure (pretty much a given when looking at the results).<br/> <br/>Good luck in future challenges!<br/> <br/>Mark</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Composition: For me the sunset area is a little too low in the scene and I think this would have looked really nice had the camera angle been slightly lower, that would have resulted in the darker area at the top being out of the frame too which i think would have added to the image, I think the Hexagon shapes are amazing and with that sunset in the background there was the making of a seriously good challenge entry here (although I think it might be a stretch for Architecture for the voters on this site). Camera Work: Everything looks to be in order, nice use of high ISO to get the shot and well retained details in the sunset. Post-Processing: Not too heavily saturated which is good as I suspect it would have been very easy to get carried away processing that sky. My Opinion: Nice out of the box thinking and I reckon in the right challenge this location has the potential to produce a shot that could finish very high, there''s some lovely shapes formed making for a very interesting capture. This one probably fell a bit in score due to the subject matter and the voters expecting to see a building or very specific types of structure (pretty much a given when looking at the results). Good luck in future challenges! Mark', '2009-06-19 12:57:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(5536601, 796964, 98339, '<td valign="top">Denise...where was this taken....such a beautiful shot, if I had been doing comments on this I would have said...just a touch to dark...but an absolutely beautiful shot...well done</td>', 'Denise...where was this taken....such a beautiful shot, if I had been doing comments on this I would have said...just a touch to dark...but an absolutely beautiful shot...well done', '2009-06-19 15:05:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(5536730, 797785, 106484, '<td valign="top">This shot intrigued me when I saw it for the first time, I decided to download it and play around with the curves...purely as a learning exercise for myself. The result was quite dramatic, just add a curves layer in Photoshop and create a gentle S curve, then add a contract layer and set it to 50.</td>', 'This shot intrigued me when I saw it for the first time, I decided to download it and play around with the curves...purely as a learning exercise for myself. The result was quite dramatic, just add a curves layer in Photoshop and create a gentle S curve, then add a contract layer and set it to 50.', '2009-06-19 16:48:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(5536746, 797093, 61651, '<td valign="top">Fascinating work - your choice for processing took an already extraordinary shot to a whole other level.  Good work, and congratulations on your blue, very well deserved.</td>', 'Fascinating work - your choice for processing took an already extraordinary shot to a whole other level.  Good work, and congratulations on your blue, very well deserved.', '2009-06-19 17:06:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(5536844, 797746, 48820, '<td valign="top">Thank you to the Critique Club and Mark-A for taking the time and giving such a honest and thorough critique.<br/>A big thanks to kenna718 and KarenNfld for taking the time during the voting to leave a note.<br/></td>', 'Thank you to the Critique Club and Mark-A for taking the time and giving such a honest and thorough critique.A big thanks to kenna718 and KarenNfld for taking the time during the voting to leave a note.', '2009-06-19 19:21:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(5537179, 797840, 96721, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/> <br/>I really liked the ground up almost extreme angle of this shot when I went through voting. I did feel a little straightening would have helped avoid the slight distraction of the tilt though. I love the way the cables lead you wonderfully up to the golden peak and the lighting on that area is gorgeous too.<br/> <br/>Camera Work:<br/> <br/>Everything looks to be in order, good shutter / ISO and aperture settings for this shot.<br/> <br/>Post-Processing:<br/> <br/>Not too heavily saturated which is good, I put the image in PS and found a Shadow / Highlights help make the image pop just a tad more, but other than that no real issues I can see.<br/> <br/>My Opinion:<br/> <br/>I liked this in voting as I mentioned above and was one of your 7 votes, that may have been an 8 without the tilt, I think you have a very interesting capture that met the challenge nicely, I am surprised you didn''t get a few more comments but sometimes that''s the way it goes.<br/> <br/>Good luck in future challenges!<br/> <br/>Mark</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Composition: I really liked the ground up almost extreme angle of this shot when I went through voting. I did feel a little straightening would have helped avoid the slight distraction of the tilt though. I love the way the cables lead you wonderfully up to the golden peak and the lighting on that area is gorgeous too. Camera Work: Everything looks to be in order, good shutter / ISO and aperture settings for this shot. Post-Processing: Not too heavily saturated which is good, I put the image in PS and found a Shadow / Highlights help make the image pop just a tad more, but other than that no real issues I can see. My Opinion: I liked this in voting as I mentioned above and was one of your 7 votes, that may have been an 8 without the tilt, I think you have a very interesting capture that met the challenge nicely, I am surprised you didn''t get a few more comments but sometimes that''s the way it goes. Good luck in future challenges! Mark', '2009-06-20 07:58:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(5537228, 796645, 15711, '<td valign="top">Amazing that this didn''t do better.  I didn''t vote but I think it''s really well done.  The angles, the perfect focus and the tonal range.  On well, who''s to say that a low score is a bad thing?</td>', 'Amazing that this didn''t do better.  I didn''t vote but I think it''s really well done.  The angles, the perfect focus and the tonal range.  On well, who''s to say that a low score is a bad thing?', '2009-06-20 09:21:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(5537582, 797218, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Initial impression: Wish I''d taken it.  Lovely, and would have been an easy 9 from me in voting.<br/> <br/>Technical: Great use of ambient light, pov works beautifully, comp could arguably be seen as a little centred but this kind of shot calls for it. Wonderful use of negative space. PP seems to be just fine.<br/> <br/>Artistic:  Actually another glance at the top part of pic reveals a slight list to the right, which enhances the sensation of vertigo.  Like the way the sky seems to form a giant ''1''.<br/> <br/>FWIW/Overall: the only change I''d make would be one pass with sharpen edges, after unsharp mask, just to give the tops of the buildings just that little more sharpness. <br/> <br/>Very good shot, you just finished out of the ribbons but in the top 10, and here that is nothing to sneeze at!  <br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Initial impression: Wish I''d taken it.  Lovely, and would have been an easy 9 from me in voting. Technical: Great use of ambient light, pov works beautifully, comp could arguably be seen as a little centred but this kind of shot calls for it. Wonderful use of negative space. PP seems to be just fine. Artistic:  Actually another glance at the top part of pic reveals a slight list to the right, which enhances the sensation of vertigo.  Like the way the sky seems to form a giant ''1''. FWIW/Overall: the only change I''d make would be one pass with sharpen edges, after unsharp mask, just to give the tops of the buildings just that little more sharpness.  Very good shot, you just finished out of the ribbons but in the top 10, and here that is nothing to sneeze at!   Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan', '2009-06-20 14:41:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(5537782, 797849, 92512, '<td valign="top">Critique Club comment;<br/>Very good intry for  Architecture  as it has lots of interesting angles and lines, and reflections.<br/>The angle at which you took this shot does well to complement the bluiders work &amp; artistry.<br/>What would some reflected light, say from a sunset''s colors done for this? What would it have done to the shadows?</td>', 'Critique Club comment;Very good intry for  Architecture  as it has lots of interesting angles and lines, and reflections.The angle at which you took this shot does well to complement the bluiders work & artistry.What would some reflected light, say from a sunset''s colors done for this? What would it have done to the shadows?', '2009-06-20 18:17:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(5537789, 798166, 92512, '<td valign="top">Critique Club comment;<br/>Very ineresting subject.<br/>I think you did well to choose an angle to add to the compostion.<br/>Did you try taking this at different times of the day when the shadows were different?<br/>What would A night photo look like?<br/>A good strong intry for this contest.<br/></td>', 'Critique Club comment;Very ineresting subject.I think you did well to choose an angle to add to the compostion.Did you try taking this at different times of the day when the shadows were different?What would A night photo look like?A good strong intry for this contest.', '2009-06-20 18:26:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(5537975, 797785, 106898, '<td valign="top">I''m getting my first lesson in photoshop on Monday. As of now I''ve just been winging it. Tweeking and trying to figure things out on my own. Besides the fact that I have a exceptionally old version on photoshop. Everyone''s comments have been quite helpful. I appreciate then greatly! Keep them coming :)</td>', 'I''m getting my first lesson in photoshop on Monday. As of now I''ve just been winging it. Tweeking and trying to figure things out on my own. Besides the fact that I have a exceptionally old version on photoshop. Everyone''s comments have been quite helpful. I appreciate then greatly! Keep them coming :)', '2009-06-20 21:55:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(5538159, 797423, 53964, '<td valign="top">I agree with the border comments and here''s why. My eye first goes to the dome, well placed on a third, then travels on the horizontal building line out to the left. When it runs out of building to look at there, it goes back and follows the archways flowing smoothly toward me. Very nice... until suddenly BAM, it runs smack into a black wall. IMO it would be more effective if my eye/imagination were allowed to extrapolate and continue that flow right off the edge of the photo.  I do like your choice of perspective and the contrast between the weathered stone and the new foliage. <br/> <br/>I hope this helps. I always hate when commenters say they don''t like something but don''t say why, or give me any tips on improvements :)</td>', 'I agree with the border comments and here''s why. My eye first goes to the dome, well placed on a third, then travels on the horizontal building line out to the left. When it runs out of building to look at there, it goes back and follows the archways flowing smoothly toward me. Very nice... until suddenly BAM, it runs smack into a black wall. IMO it would be more effective if my eye/imagination were allowed to extrapolate and continue that flow right off the edge of the photo.  I do like your choice of perspective and the contrast between the weathered stone and the new foliage.  I hope this helps. I always hate when commenters say they don''t like something but don''t say why, or give me any tips on improvements :)', '2009-06-21 02:34:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(5538182, 796924, 59684, '<td valign="top">Thx Mark for your critique.</td>', 'Thx Mark for your critique.', '2009-06-21 03:40:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(5538260, 796557, 96721, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/> <br/>I really this shot in voting, I love the light and dark contrasts of the upright lines I think they demand a longer look than one might normally give to an image. My one small concern is the slight lean on the left which introduces a line at the top and makes the building look slightly distracting in the lower quarter, however looking at the right side of the image I can see your straighting options were limited as any tilt on that side would most likely have been even more noticable.<br/> <br/>Camera Work:<br/> <br/>Nothing obviously wrong with your camera settings that I can see.<br/> <br/>Post-Processing:<br/> <br/>Love the fact you have left this almost muted in saturation those blues in the window reflections are simply glorious, I might have been tempted to reduce the reds a bit and try and take out some of the weight of the building reflected on the lower right but that would also have affected the crane so that''s a tough choice as the crane IMHO really adds something to the shot. I also like the fact that you have not oversharpened the image which allows for an almost smooth feel to the whole shot.<br/> <br/>My Opinion:<br/> <br/>I really like your eye, I think you captured a really intriguing image and I look forward to seeing more of you work entered in to challenges. Congratulations on a fine first entry.<br/> <br/>Good luck in future challenges!<br/> <br/>Mark</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Composition: I really this shot in voting, I love the light and dark contrasts of the upright lines I think they demand a longer look than one might normally give to an image. My one small concern is the slight lean on the left which introduces a line at the top and makes the building look slightly distracting in the lower quarter, however looking at the right side of the image I can see your straighting options were limited as any tilt on that side would most likely have been even more noticable. Camera Work: Nothing obviously wrong with your camera settings that I can see. Post-Processing: Love the fact you have left this almost muted in saturation those blues in the window reflections are simply glorious, I might have been tempted to reduce the reds a bit and try and take out some of the weight of the building reflected on the lower right but that would also have affected the crane so that''s a tough choice as the crane IMHO really adds something to the shot. I also like the fact that you have not oversharpened the image which allows for an almost smooth feel to the whole shot. My Opinion: I really like your eye, I think you captured a really intriguing image and I look forward to seeing more of you work entered in to challenges. Congratulations on a fine first entry. Good luck in future challenges! Mark', '2009-06-21 08:28:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(5538309, 796873, 96721, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Composition:<br/> <br/>I think Citadels comment is pretty much on the money really although I think I can see what you were going for, the lamp feels like it should sit there nicely as it''s on the 3rds line and so should be asthetically pleasing, but unfortunately something doesn''t work with it, the pole doesn''t bother me that much but I think it''s the lamp heads that spoil the shot - they definitely demand a lot of attention.<br/> <br/>Maybe a higher position with this same idea would work better, so the top of the building was dominent in the scene this would give more weight to the building and take a lot of it away from the lamp.<br/> <br/>Camera Work:<br/> <br/>Seems ok, I guess one could argue that the lamp looks totally sharp and the windows of the building are maybe a little soft, I wonder if your intention was actually on the lamp as architecture? If this is the case I think a tiny DOF would have worked better and you know what, I think that may have worked.<br/> <br/>Post-Processing:<br/> <br/>Nice saturation levels, I would possibly have applied a shadow / highlight on the image to improve some of the detail in the flowers and deepen the sky just a touch.<br/> <br/>My Opinion:<br/> <br/>Quite a nice idea and congratulations on your first entry I hope to see more of your work as you progress on the site.<br/> <br/>Hope this was of some use, should you wish to discuss any part of the critique further please feel free to PM me.<br/> <br/>Good luck in future challenges!<br/> <br/>Mark</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Composition: I think Citadels comment is pretty much on the money really although I think I can see what you were going for, the lamp feels like it should sit there nicely as it''s on the 3rds line and so should be asthetically pleasing, but unfortunately something doesn''t work with it, the pole doesn''t bother me that much but I think it''s the lamp heads that spoil the shot - they definitely demand a lot of attention. Maybe a higher position with this same idea would work better, so the top of the building was dominent in the scene this would give more weight to the building and take a lot of it away from the lamp. Camera Work: Seems ok, I guess one could argue that the lamp looks totally sharp and the windows of the building are maybe a little soft, I wonder if your intention was actually on the lamp as architecture? If this is the case I think a tiny DOF would have worked better and you know what, I think that may have worked. Post-Processing: Nice saturation levels, I would possibly have applied a shadow / highlight on the image to improve some of the detail in the flowers and deepen the sky just a touch. My Opinion: Quite a nice idea and congratulations on your first entry I hope to see more of your work as you progress on the site. Hope this was of some use, should you wish to discuss any part of the critique further please feel free to PM me. Good luck in future challenges! Mark', '2009-06-21 10:19:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(5539183, 798005, 64844, '<td valign="top">This was one my favorite ones in this challenge! Unfortunately I got so busy over here that I couldn''t vote nor participate! Just looked at all briefly as I so wished I could have entered one of my own...<br/>What a great building and wonderful use of lensbaby!</td>', 'This was one my favorite ones in this challenge! Unfortunately I got so busy over here that I couldn''t vote nor participate! Just looked at all briefly as I so wished I could have entered one of my own...What a great building and wonderful use of lensbaby!', '2009-06-22 05:50:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(5539768, 796742, 96509, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the critic! I still have much to learn!<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Mark-A:</b></div><hr/><i> Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/>One thing I am not sure about is did you achieve this shot through shooting in to a mirrored surface?<br/></i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I''m not sure to understand the question, the objective that appear in the picture is looking towards the building, the camera is located between the building and this objective. The lenses inside the objective are performing as mirrors but there is no other reflective surface used in the shot. I hope this explain better how I prepare the shot.<br/> <br/>Max<br/></td>', 'Thanks for the critic! I still have much to learn! Originally posted by Mark-A: Greetings from the Critique Club :)One thing I am not sure about is did you achieve this shot through shooting in to a mirrored surface?  I''m not sure to understand the question, the objective that appear in the picture is looking towards the building, the camera is located between the building and this objective. The lenses inside the objective are performing as mirrors but there is no other reflective surface used in the shot. I hope this explain better how I prepare the shot. Max', '2009-06-22 14:13:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(5540173, 797653, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Initial impression: Lovely shot, great clean sweeping lines, as someone else said looks pristine.  Had I voted, a 9 or 10, because you are not relying on the people to carry the shot.<br/> <br/>Technical: Great pov, the comp draws the eye up to the soaring roof and the pavilion, finally you notice the people.  Very clever! Lighting looks ambient and bang-on for the shot.<br/> <br/>Artistic: Terrific sense of a story being told in such an intriguing location.  Made me think of a scene before the new 007, James Blond, bursts onto the scene and trashes the place!  Pity you didn''t finish higher, but a top-20 finish is very good too.<br/> <br/>Overall: Glad you did so little editing. Good work, keep shooting!<br/> <br/>Feel free to PM me with any questions,<br/> <br/>Susan<br/> <br/>(Or just send me a few tins of Twiglets out of gratitude ;-)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Initial impression: Lovely shot, great clean sweeping lines, as someone else said looks pristine.  Had I voted, a 9 or 10, because you are not relying on the people to carry the shot. Technical: Great pov, the comp draws the eye up to the soaring roof and the pavilion, finally you notice the people.  Very clever! Lighting looks ambient and bang-on for the shot. Artistic: Terrific sense of a story being told in such an intriguing location.  Made me think of a scene before the new 007, James Blond, bursts onto the scene and trashes the place!  Pity you didn''t finish higher, but a top-20 finish is very good too. Overall: Glad you did so little editing. Good work, keep shooting! Feel free to PM me with any questions, Susan (Or just send me a few tins of Twiglets out of gratitude ;-)', '2009-06-22 20:06:59.000000', NULL, 0),
